[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-011
TITLE: Message ownership operations
STATEMENT: >>>
Ownership operations apply to dynamic ``ZMsg`` values. Normal post transfers
the caller reference; ``postpone`` adds a reference; dequeue transfers the
Mailbox reference to the receiver; final release returns the block to its
originating pool.

.. code-block:: c

   void z_message_retain(ZMsg const *message);
   void z_message_release(ZMsg const *message);

For a dynamic Message, retain adds one reference, release removes one
reference, and the final release returns the block to its originating pool.
Reference-count overflow and release without a dynamic reference are panic
conditions. For a static immutable Message, retain and release are no-ops;
they do not inspect or change a claim count and never return the Message to a
pool. Callers may therefore use the same cleanup path for either Message kind
without first identifying which kind they received.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-011
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-012
TITLE: Message pool inputs and limits
STATEMENT: >>>
Pool initialization accepts caller storage, storage size, block size, and block
count. Storage shall be non-null, aligned to ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``, and at
least ``block_size * block_count`` bytes. ``block_size`` shall be nonzero,
large enough for the private envelope plus the largest payload class assigned
to the pool, and a multiple of ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``. ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``
shall be at least the alignment required by ``max_align_t``.

The pool set surface is:

.. code-block:: c

   typedef struct {
       ZMessagePool *pools;
       uint16_t count;
   } ZMessagePoolSet;

   void z_message_pool_set_init(ZMessagePoolSet *pool_set,
                                ZMessagePool *pools,
                                uint16_t count);

Each element of ``pools`` shall have been initialized by
``z_message_pool_init``. The set retains the caller-owned array for the set's
lifetime; the array shall not be reordered or modified while Messages may be
allocated from the set.

A requested allocation includes the private envelope and payload. The message
service selects the smallest available block size that can contain that total;
equal block sizes are selected in caller-supplied array order. If no suitable
pool has a free block, the panic boundary is used.

.. code-block:: c

   void z_message_pool_init(ZMessagePool *pool,
                            void *storage,
                            size_t storage_size,
                            size_t block_size,
                            uint16_t block_count);

Exhaustion, invalid alignment, insufficient storage, invalid sizing, an empty
pool set, and an unavailable pointer use the panic boundary rather than
returning a null pointer or ordinary exhaustion result.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-010
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-013
TITLE: Mailbox operations
STATEMENT: >>>
Inputs include a non-null caller-owned pointer ring and nonzero capacity. The
ring shall not alias the Mailbox object or another live Mailbox ring. A Mailbox
may be assigned to a registered Reactor as its scheduler-visible inbox or may
remain unassigned as an application-owned local/deferred queue. The Mailbox
surface is:

.. code-block:: c

   void z_mailbox_init(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                       ZMsg const **slots,
                       uint16_t capacity);
    void z_mailbox_post(ZMailbox *mailbox, ZMsg **message);
   void z_mailbox_postpone(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                           ZMsg const *message);
    void z_mailbox_post_front(ZMailbox *mailbox, ZMsg **message);
   ZResult z_mailbox_recall(ZMailbox *destination,
                            ZMailbox *source);
   ZResult z_mailbox_take(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                          ZMsg const **message_out);

   uint16_t z_mailbox_count(ZMailbox const *mailbox);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_capacity(ZMailbox const *mailbox);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_free(ZMailbox const *mailbox);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_min_free(ZMailbox const *mailbox);

   typedef ZBool (*ZMsgPredicate)(ZMsg const *message, void *context);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_remove_if(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                                ZMsgPredicate predicate,
                                void *context);

Empty ``take`` returns ``Z_EMPTY`` without writing its output. Full enqueue
uses the panic boundary. ``post_front`` places the new Message immediately
before the current next Message; repeated front posts are therefore taken in
last-in-first-out order. ``recall`` moves exactly one newest Message from an
unassigned source Mailbox to the front of the destination Mailbox, transferring
queue ownership without changing claims; it returns ``Z_EMPTY`` when the source
is empty, rejects a Kernel-managed source, and uses the panic boundary if the
destination is full. ``remove_if``
evaluates its predicate once per queued
entry in delivery order, preserves the order of retained entries, returns the
number removed, and releases each removed dynamic Message claim. A null
predicate is an invalid argument and invokes the panic handler. The minimum
free-space observation is initialized to capacity and records the lowest free
space reached since Mailbox initialization. Operations on an unassigned local
Mailbox never update a Kernel runnable identity and never dispatch Messages
automatically. An application may defer a Message received by a handler
with ``z_mailbox_postpone`` and later call ``z_mailbox_take`` when the
owning Machine reaches the appropriate state. For a dynamic Message, the
application then owns the returned claim and releases it after processing. For
a static Message, the application receives a reference with no claim; calling
``z_message_release`` after processing is still valid and is a no-op. The
``postpone`` name means that the Message is retained for later handling; the
Message storage is shared between the existing caller reference and the new
Mailbox reference for dynamic Messages, while static Messages simply share the
same reference. The payload is not copied.
``z_mailbox_postpone`` is only valid for an unassigned Mailbox; passing a
Kernel-managed Mailbox is an invalid argument. Actor and Reactor Mailboxes
must use normal post or front-post operations so scheduling ownership remains
with the Kernel.

.. code-block:: c

   ZMailbox deferred;
   ZMsg const *deferred_slots[4];
   z_mailbox_init(&deferred, deferred_slots, 4);

    /* In a handler that must defer the current borrowed Message. */
   z_mailbox_postpone(&deferred, message);

   /* In the application-selected state that is ready to process it. */
   ZMsg const *deferred_message;
   if (z_mailbox_take(&deferred, &deferred_message) == Z_OK) {
       z_machine_dispatch(machine, deferred_message);
       z_message_release(deferred_message);
   }
<<<
