  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-023
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-021
TITLE: Machine action vocabulary
STATEMENT: >>>
The action vocabulary and callback types are:

.. code-block:: c

   typedef enum {
       Z_ACTION_UNHANDLED,
       Z_ACTION_HANDLED,
       Z_ACTION_TRANSITION
   } ZActionKind;

   typedef struct {
       ZActionKind kind;
       ZStateNode const *target;
   } ZAction;

   typedef ZAction (*ZStateHandler)(void *context,
                                    ZMsg const *message);
   typedef void (*ZStateHook)(void *context);

Handlers return an action; hooks return no value. ``ZAction.target`` shall be
null for ``Z_ACTION_UNHANDLED`` and ``Z_ACTION_HANDLED`` and non-null for
``Z_ACTION_TRANSITION``. Any other combination is a state violation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-020
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-022
TITLE: Reactor and lifecycle surface
STATEMENT: >>>
Reactor configuration carries one primary Machine pointer, a Mailbox pointer,
and an application context. Registration returns a zero-based ``ZReactorId``.
The application context may contain auxiliary Machines, including submachines,
created and managed by application code. The framework does not create,
register, schedule, or remove those auxiliary Machines; application code may
dispatch the same read-only Message to them synchronously as part of its
Reactor handling.

Because ``ZReactor`` is a complete public structure, an application may embed
it as the first member of a larger application-defined structure and pass the
address of that embedded member to framework APIs. The framework manages only
the embedded ``ZReactor`` portion; application code owns the extension state and
any auxiliary Machines or submachines.

.. code-block:: c

   typedef struct {
       ZMachine *machine;
       ZMailbox *mailbox;
       void *context;
   } ZReactorConfig;

   void z_reactor_init(ZReactor *reactor,
                       ZReactorConfig const *config);
   ZReactorId z_reactor_register(ZKernel *kernel,
                                 ZReactor *reactor,
                                 ZReactorId id);
   void z_reactor_start(ZKernel *kernel, ZReactorId id);

Initialization and registration failures use the panic boundary. Registration
is valid only for a constructed Reactor, an in-range unoccupied identity, and
assigns that identity exactly once; the identity remains registered for the
Kernel lifetime. Start is valid only for a registered Reactor and may be called
once. There is no stopped or removed Reactor state in Phase 1. Posting to an
unregistered identity is an invalid argument and invokes the panic handler.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-030
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-032
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Actor convenience composition

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-023
TITLE: Actor composition surface
STATEMENT: >>>
``ZActor`` is a convenience composition of one ``ZReactor``, one
``ZMailbox``, and one ``ZMachine``. Its initialization accepts a state-machine
root, application context, and caller-owned Mailbox slot storage, then
initializes the three embedded objects with consistent references. Actor
registration and start forward to the existing Reactor lifecycle and do not
introduce a separate scheduler entity, allocation policy, or stop operation.

.. code-block:: c

   void z_actor_init(ZActor *actor,
                     ZStateNode const *root,
                     void *context,
                     ZMsg const **mailbox_slots,
                     uint16_t mailbox_capacity);
   ZReactorId z_actor_register(ZKernel *kernel,
                               ZActor *actor,
                               ZReactorId id);
   void z_actor_start(ZActor *actor);
   #define Z_MSG_POST(actor_, msg_) \
       do { \
           ZMsg *zef_post_message_ = (ZMsg *)(msg_); \
           z_mailbox_post(&(actor_)->mailbox, &zef_post_message_); \
           (msg_) = zef_post_message_; \
       } while (0)

The Actor owns the embedded framework objects but the application owns the
state graph, context, and Mailbox slot storage. The embedded Mailbox remains
the normal posting and deferred-queue interface, and the embedded Reactor
retains the assigned identity and priority semantics.
``Z_MSG_POST(actor, msg)`` is the typed composition convenience for the normal
actor destination; it transfers ``*msg`` to the actor Mailbox and leaves the
caller pointer null. The variable may point to either a dynamic or static
Message; static Messages carry no claim. Direct Mailbox posting remains
available for standalone or non-Actor destinations.
``Z_MSG_POST`` is ISR-safe because it delegates to the same bounded,
critical-section-protected ``z_mailbox_post`` operation; there is no separate
Actor-specific ISR posting API.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-034
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Kernel, topics, and Clock

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-030
TITLE: Kernel surface
STATEMENT: >>>
The Port is supplied during Kernel initialization. The finite scheduler surface
is:

.. code-block:: c

   typedef enum {
       Z_NO_WORK,
       Z_WORK_DONE
   } ZRunResult;

   void z_kernel_init(ZKernel *kernel, ZPort const *port);
   ZRunResult z_kernel_run_one(ZKernel *kernel);
   void z_kernel_run_forever(ZKernel *kernel);
   ZResult z_kernel_current_priority(ZKernel const *kernel,
                                     ZPriority *priority_out);

``z_kernel_run_one`` dispatches at most one Message. ``run_forever`` calls the
Port idle operation whenever ``run_one`` reports no work. During a dispatch,
``z_kernel_current_priority`` returns ``Z_OK`` and writes the active priority;
when no dispatch is active it returns ``Z_NOT_RUNNING`` without writing the
output.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-040
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-041
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-031
TITLE: Topic surface
STATEMENT: >>>
A topic identifies a documented payload schema. A TopicTable is bound to one
Kernel at initialization so it can validate Reactor identities and use their
strict identity priorities for publication ordering. Publication accepts a
building ``ZMsg`` and returns a delivery count on normal completion.

.. code-block:: c

   void z_topic_table_init(ZTopicTable *table,
                           ZKernel *kernel);
   void z_topic_subscribe(ZTopicTable *table,
                          ZTopic topic,
                          ZReactorId reactor_id);
   ZResult z_topic_unsubscribe(ZTopicTable *table,
                               ZTopic topic,
                               ZReactorId reactor_id);
   uint16_t z_topic_publish(ZKernel *kernel,
                            ZTopicTable *table,
                            ZMsg *message);

Subscription changes and publication snapshots use the global Port critical-section
contract. The Kernel argument to publication shall be the Kernel bound to the
TopicTable. A publication observes exactly the subscription snapshot taken in
that critical section; later changes do not affect it. Repeating an existing
subscription is idempotent. Subscribing an identity that is not registered in
the bound Kernel is invalid. An absent unsubscribe returns ``Z_NOT_FOUND``;
out-of-range topics and Reactor identities are invalid arguments and invoke
the panic handler.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-050
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-032
TITLE: Clock and Timer surface
STATEMENT: >>>
Timer inputs include a destination Reactor identity, a timer-owned immutable
Message, delay, and period. The Clock and Timer surface is:

.. code-block:: c

   void z_clock_init(ZClock *clock, ZKernel *kernel);
   void z_timer_init_topic(ZTimer *timer,
                           ZClock *clock,
                           ZReactorId destination,
                           ZTopic topic);
   void z_timer_init_payload(ZTimer *timer,
                             ZClock *clock,
                             ZReactorId destination,
                             ZMessagePoolSet *pool_set,
                             ZTopic topic,
                             void const *payload,
                             size_t payload_size);
   void z_timer_arm(ZTimer *timer, ZTick delay, ZTick period);
   ZResult z_timer_stop(ZTimer *timer, ZBool clear_pending);
   ZResult z_timer_destroy(ZTimer *timer);
   void z_clock_tick(ZClock *clock);

``z_timer_init_payload`` copies the supplied payload into one Timer-owned
dynamic Message and retains its base claim for the Timer's initialized
lifetime, including while stopped or expired. The Timer retains an additional
claim for each pending dynamic delivery. The ``z_timer_init_topic`` form
creates a Timer-owned immutable zero-payload Message for the supplied topic and
requires no Message pool.

Stopping an already disarmed Timer returns ``Z_NOT_RUNNING``. Timer expiration
posts a Message and does not invoke application handlers from tick context.
Timers expiring on the same tick are posted in arm order. Tick arithmetic is
unsigned and modular; zero delay expires on the next tick, and delays and
periods are limited to the configured less-than-half-range maximum.
``z_timer_stop(timer, 1)`` stops the Timer and removes its pending private
Message deliveries without removing unrelated Mailbox entries, while keeping
the Timer reusable. ``z_timer_destroy`` performs the same removal but also
releases the Timer's retained dynamic Message claim and makes the Timer
terminal. It returns ``Z_NOT_RUNNING`` when the Timer was already destroyed.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-060
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-061
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Port and verification surface

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-040
TITLE: Internal port adapter
STATEMENT: >>>
The target Port supplies global critical-section operations used by all
framework objects, plus Kernel scheduler callbacks for deferred rescheduling
and idle behavior. Critical sections are processor-global and do not belong to
one Kernel or Mailbox.

.. code-block:: c

   typedef uint32_t ZCriticalState;

   ZCriticalState z_port_critical_enter(void);
   void z_port_critical_leave(ZCriticalState state);

   struct ZPort {
       void (*request_reschedule)(void);
       void (*idle)(void);
   };

Critical entry and leave restore the saved state and support nesting. The
framework uses them around scheduler state, Mailbox ring state, Message claim
counts, and Message-pool free-list mutations. These hooks do not require a
Kernel and therefore also protect unregistered Mailboxes and standalone
Message pools. Fatal conditions call the
application-provided ``z_panic`` function; the panic function is not a Port
callback. Target-specific context-switching operations are outside this
surface until the shared-stack preemptive port is included in a phase
baseline.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-070
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-071
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-042
TITLE: Shared-stack preemption assumptions
STATEMENT: >>>
The shared-stack preemptive target contract shall use one linker-provided,
8-byte-aligned execution stack for Reactor activation and shall not require a
stack-storage field in ``ZReactor``. A target Port shall reserve the lowest
system-handler priority for deferred rescheduling, shall use the target's
available global interrupt mask for Kernel critical sections, and shall expose
the configured stack bound and observed high-water mark to target diagnostics.
Reactor handlers shall be non-blocking, shall not retain automatic-storage
addresses across a dispatch boundary, and shall not require independent saved
continuations. The host Port may model eligibility and nested activation, but
cannot claim to verify target exception-frame construction or exception return.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-071
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-IF-040
- TYPE: File
  FORMAT: Sourcecode
  PATH: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-041
TITLE: Public API interrupt-context contract
STATEMENT: >>>
The public API shall classify every application-callable function and macro as
ISR-safe or thread-only. Phase 1 shall support these operations from interrupt
or handler context:

* ``z_message_begin``;
* ``z_message_payload_mut`` and ``z_message_payload_mut_checked``;
* ``z_mailbox_post``;
* ``z_mailbox_post_front``;
* ``z_mailbox_postpone``; and
* ``z_topic_publish``.

Those operations shall be bounded, shall not allocate from a general-purpose
allocator or block, and shall use the documented critical-section and ownership
rules. The ``Z_MSG_BEGIN`` and ``Z_MSG_PAYLOAD_MUT`` macros inherit the
ISR-safe classification of their fixed-pool construction functions, and
``Z_MSG_POST`` inherits the ISR-safe classification of ``z_mailbox_post``.
Message
read access, retain/release, pool initialization, Mailbox removal and take,
subscription changes, Machine, Reactor, Kernel, Clock, and Timer operations
are thread-only in Phase 1. A later phase may add a separately named ISR
variant for operations that are currently thread-only, but no unclassified application API is permitted. Internal Port
callbacks are outside this application API classification.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-070
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-050
TITLE: Consumer verification surface
STATEMENT: >>>
Public-header verification uses consumer translation units that include public
headers and link the built library. Every type, function, macro, and constant
has a compile test. Every normative runtime behavior has a behavior test;
documentation-only, provenance, and license items have a review record with
explicit pass/fail criteria. Each evidence item has a traceability link to
its requirement UID.
<<<
